<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- noupdate="0" DELIBERATELY (W13): a frozen file means P3b and P4 could never
     move this record without shipping a migration, and both of them touch the
     Workforce rail. The three phases before this one each paid for that lesson
     once (W13.1/W27). -->
<odoo noupdate="0">

    <!-- WORKFORCE › Mission Control — the whole section, in one door.

         Seven items (Today 10 · Schedule 20 · Time 30 · Time Off 40 ·
         Overtime 50 · Trips 60 · Team Approvals 70) become this one, and they
         all move into the 900 retired band: a deactivated item still OCCUPIES
         its sequence for W8's uniqueness rule, and this record takes 10 (W18).
         None of the seven client actions is touched — every cockpit is still
         registered and still reachable at its own URL.

         P3b renames this item "Workforce" -> "Mission Control". The section
         header already says WORKFORCE, so "WORKFORCE > Workforce" read as a
         stutter; "WORKFORCE > Mission Control" reads as intended. The action
         itself keeps the name "Workforce", because that is what the breadcrumb
         and the section are about.

         W28 (a label is unique across the WHOLE sidebar table, not just its
         section): no other `pb.sidebar.item` in any module carries either
         label. "Mission Control" appears nowhere else in the codebase's
         sidebar data, and Formula Studio's own Command Center is a ~K palette
         inside a cockpit, not a rail entry — deliberately NOT reused as a name
         here, because two things called Command Center would be two things.

         The record is `noupdate="0"` and its stored `ir_model_data.noupdate`
         flag was asserted clear by P3a, so a plain `-u pb_mission` really does
         move it — which W13.1 says must still be checked in the DATABASE
         afterwards, and `test_sidebar.py` is what checks it.

         UNGATED, on purpose. The seven it replaces had five different gates
         between them and TWO of them — Trips and Team Approvals — were open to
         every internal user, so any gate here would take a door away from
         somebody who has one today. Instead, the shell's rail asks each lens's
         own question client-side (`LENSES[].groups` in pb_mission.js) and shows
         only the lenses this persona could actually read, so nobody is offered
         a surface that would answer with an AccessError. That is a visibility
         hint, not a security boundary: every facade still enforces its own gate
         server-side (W12). -->
    <record id="item_workforce" model="pb.sidebar.item">
        <field name="section_id" ref="pb_sidebar.sec_workforce"/>
        <field name="name">Mission Control</field>
        <field name="icon">compass</field>
        <field name="sequence">10</field>
        <field name="action_xmlid">pb_mission.action_pb_workforce</field>
        <field name="action_tag">pb_workforce</field>
        <!-- The seven absorbed cockpits keep their client actions (W18), so a
             bookmark, a stray doAction or the shell's own standalone fallback
             must light THIS entry rather than leaving the rail with nothing
             selected. `shift_planning_grid` and the P1a trio ride along for the
             same reason: they were already matched by the items being retired,
             and dropping them here would quietly lose that. -->
        <field name="match_action_tags">pb_workforce,pb_today,pb_schedule,pb_time_hub,pb_timeoff,pb_ot_desk,pb_trips,pb_team,shift_planning_grid,pb_attendance_weekgrid,pb_attendance_flow,attendance_live,pb_driver_map</field>
    </record>

</odoo>
